<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">County Louth</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">County Louth is the smallest county in Ireland, on the east coast in the province of Leinster, with Dundalk and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogheda">Drogheda</a> as its main towns. Part of the kingdom of Airgialla in the 12th century, it was settled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-normans">Anglo-Normans</a> from the late 12th century and lay within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-pale">English Pale</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellifont-abbey">Mellifont Abbey</a> and the early monastery of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monasterboice">Monasterboice</a> are in the county.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Louth is the smallest of Ireland's 32 counties by area, at 826 square kilometres, and is known colloquially as the Wee County. It is bounded by Meath to the south, Monaghan to the west, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armagh">Armagh</a> to the north and Down to the north-east across Carlingford Lough, and by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/irish-sea">Irish Sea</a> to the east. It is the northernmost county in Leinster and the only one in the province to share a border with Northern Ireland. The county is named after the village of Louth, which takes its name from Lugh, a god of the ancient Irish.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The county is the setting of part of the epic Táin Bó Cúailnge. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Vikings</a> left their mark in the name of Carlingford Lough and founded a longphort at Annagassan in the ninth century. At that time Louth consisted of three sub-kingdoms: Conaille, Fir Rois and Fir Arda Ciannachta. Early in the 12th century the whole area became part of the O'Carroll kingdom of Airgíalla under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donnchad-ua-cerbaill">Donnchad Ua Cerbaill</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> occupied it in the 1180s and formed the County of Oriel, in time distinguishing 'English' Oriel, the later Louth, from 'Irish' Oriel, which passed to the McMahon lordship of Airgíalla.</p><p>In 1316 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-bruce">Edward Bruce</a> was crowned on the hill of Maledon near Dundalk, and his Scottish army laid waste much of the Anglo-Norman colony, including Ardee and Dundalk, before it was defeated and Bruce killed at the Battle of Faughart. In 1412 a royal charter united the two towns of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogheda">Drogheda</a> as a county in its own right. Louth was considered part of Ulster until 1596, when a conference at Faughart led to its becoming part of Leinster.</p><p>Because Louth lay on the main route to the Gap of the North, the 16th and 17th centuries brought many skirmishes. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogheda">Drogheda</a> in 1649 and slaughtered the Royalist garrison and hundreds of citizens. Late in the same century the armies of James II and William of Orange faced each other in south Louth before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-boyne">Battle of the Boyne</a>, fought 3 kilometres west of Drogheda. In 1798 several leaders of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/society-of-united-irishmen">United Irishmen</a> came from the county, and were betrayed by informers; several were hanged.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dundalk is the county town and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drogheda">Drogheda</a> is the larger of the two main towns; the county had 139,703 people at the 2022 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>. Its historic sites include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mellifont-abbey">Mellifont Abbey</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monasterboice">Monasterboice</a>, the St Mary Magdalene Dominican Friary and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-king-of-england">King John</a>'s Castle at Carlingford. The Cooley Peninsula was the last Gaeltacht outpost in Leinster, and its dialect, Gaeilge Oriel, died out with its last native speaker in 1960.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “County Louth” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Airgialla means 'hostage-givers', and the name describes a group of peoples in mid-Ulster, in modern Armagh, Monaghan, Tyrone, Fermanagh and <b>Louth</b>, who owed hostages and military service to the Uí Néill.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-airgialla">Kingdom of Airgialla</a></li></ul></section>
